      "sourceFigureEntries": 2,
      "sourceCaptionEntries": 2,
      "sectionBoundaries": [
        {
          "before": "At many power stations on Earth, it is the Lorentz force that moves electrons in moving conductors.",
          "after": "We now calculate the induced EMF in a rectangular circuit in a uniform magnetic field (Figure 290). Let one side of the circuit, $AB=l$, slide at a constant velocity $\\vec{v}$ along sides $BC$ and $AD$.\nThe conductor AB remains parallel to side $CD$ at all times. The magnetic induction $\\vec{B}$ is perpendicular to conductor $AB$ and makes an angle $\\alpha$ with its velocity."
        },
        {
          "before": "The EMF in the other conductors of the circuit is zero because they are not moving. The EMF in the entire circuit $ABCD$ therefore equals $\\mathcal{E}_i$ in (12-6).\nIf the speed $v$ is constant, the EMF remains constant. The current then increases because the total resistance of the circuit decreases as conductor $AB$ moves to the right.",
          "after": "The induced EMF can also be calculated using the law of electromagnetic induction (12-4). The magnetic flux through circuit $ABCD$ is\n$\\Phi=BS\\cos(90^\\circ-\\alpha)=BS\\sin\\alpha$.\nHere, $90^\\circ-\\alpha$ is the angle between vector $\\vec{B}$ and the normal $\\vec{n}$ to the surface of the circuit (Figure 291), and $S$ is the area of circuit $ABCD$. If, at the initial instant $(t=0)$, the distance between conductors $AB$ and $CD$ is $BC$ (Figure 290), then the area changes as the conductor moves according to"
        },
        {
          "before": "This is the same result obtained earlier in formula (12-6).",
          "after": "If the entire circuit $ABCD$ moves in a uniform magnetic field while maintaining its orientation relative to vector $\\vec{B}$, the induced EMF in the circuit is zero because the magnetic flux through its area does not change.\nThis can also be explained as follows: when the circuit moves, forces act on the electrons in conductors $AB$ and $CD$, directed from $B$ to $A$ and from $C$ to $D$, respectively. The total work done by these forces in one clockwise or counterclockwise trip around the circuit is zero."
        }
      ],
